{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The user has provided me with a static DDL review task. The task states that this is a static review, I do not have any tools, and I cannot execute any scripts. I can only judge each item based on the specification knowledge I have mastered.\n\nNote: this is a simulated scenario; the prompt explicitly says \"this is a static review: you do not have any tools, cannot execute any scripts, please judge item by item based solely on the specification knowledge you possess.\" So I should not call any tools, and instead directly perform the review.\n\nLet me carefully review the 5 tables in example.sql:\n\n**Table 1: t_order_bad_index**\n\nIndexes:\n- PRIMARY KEY (id)\n- KEY ix_order_no (order_no)\n- KEY ix_buyer_id (buyer_id)\n- KEY ix_id_status (id, order_status) — violation: id already has a primary key index; building a composite index starting with id is a duplicate\n- KEY ix_buyer_status (buyer_id, order_status) — also note that ix_buyer_id (buyer_id) is a prefix of ix_buyer_status (buyer_id, order_status), so ix_buyer_id is redundant\n- KEY ix_create_time (create_time)\n- KEY ix_status_time (order_status, create_time)\n- KEY ix_extra1 (extra_field1) — referenced field extra_field1 does not exist! This will actually fail to execute. But from a specification perspective: the index references a non-existent field.\n- KEY ix_extra2 (extra_field2) — likewise, the field does not exist\n- KEY ix_extra3 (extra_field3) — likewise, the field does not exist\n\nRule basis: the spec document provides \"index naming whether it complies with the ix_/uk_ specification (already checked by the script)\" and \"index validity (requires manual running of EXPLAIN)\". The spec documents do not explicitly list the rules of \"no duplicate index on primary key id\" and \"number of composite index fields ≤ 5\". However, the comments in the file claim these are violations.\n\nWait, I need to review based on the spec document. The spec document ddl-manual-rules.md covers:\n1. Naming semantics (mandatory): attribute-level naming, content-perspective naming, generalized words, er/pty distinction, pinyin, table-name entity\n2. Comment quality (mandatory): necessity of parenthetical supplementary information, field-comment correspondence\n3. Design reasonableness (recommended): plural forms, string length, data row length, NOT NULL + default value\n4. Prohibited statements (mandatory): triggers, stored procedures, views/custom functions\n5. Requires runtime verification (mandatory): index validity (EXPLAIN)\n\nThe SKILL.md mentions that ddl_check.py already checks: prohibited field types, required fields, logical deletion del_flag, index naming (ix_/uk_ prefix). From the \"review checklist\":\n- Index naming complies with ix_/uk_ specification\n- Number of index fields? The checklist does not mention a limit of ≤5 composite index fields. But the file comments say \"violation 2: composite index fields > 5\".\n\nHmm, my task is to \"list, item by item, the issues that genuinely violate the spec.\" I need to judge based on the spec, not based on the hints in the file comments. Some things in the comments may indeed be violations, but they may not be covered by the spec (such as composite index field count limit — not mentioned in the spec).\n\nBut wait, the user said \"help me review this CREATE TABLE statement and see whether the index design has any problems.\" Focus is on index design. The given spec docs are the basis for review.\n\nLet me strictly check each table item by item according to the \"review checklist\":\n\n**Table 1: t_order_bad_index**\n1. Table name: t_order_bad_index — centered on the core entity order; \"bad_index\" is a naming issue, but this may just be for the test case. No pinyin, no generalized words, no plural. OK.\n2. Table comment: '订单表-索引设计不当' — exists, ≤64 characters. OK.\n3. Field names: order_no, buyer_id, order_status, creator_id, etc. — all OK.\n4. Field comments: OK.\n5. Field types: bigint(20), varchar(36), tinyint(4), datetime — these are OK (the spec does not say these are prohibited types; typical prohibited types are float/double, enum, etc.).\n6. Required fields: id/creator_id/create_time/last_updater_id/last_update_time are all present. OK.\n7. Logical deletion: del_flag comment '删除标志[0-否,1-是]' format is correct. OK.\n8. Index naming: all start with ix_. Compliant.\n9. Index validity: EXPLAIN needs to be run. Static review cannot verify. But from the design perspective:\n   - KEY ix_id_status (id, order_status): the leading column is the primary key id; id already has a PRIMARY KEY, so this index is redundant. This belongs to \"index design\" issues. Is there a corresponding rule in the spec? The spec document has no explicit rule text saying \"no redundant index on primary key\". But the comment in the file claims this is a violation. The task requires reporting \"genuinely spec-violating issues\". Hmm.\n   - KEY ix_buyer_status (buyer_id, order_status) and KEY ix_buyer_id (buyer_id): the leftmost prefix duplicates; ix_buyer_id is redundant. Same issue.\n   - KEY ix_extra1 (extra_field1): the indexed field extra_field1 does not exist in the table definition! This is a hard error. Although the spec document has no explicit rule, the \"index validity\" rule says index fields must match query conditions. A field not existing is a design error. From the spec perspective it can be classified under \"index validity\" or \"referencing non-existent field\".\n10. Design reasonableness: field count ≤40 OK; single row 8+36+8+1+36+5(datetime 5 bytes)+36+5+1≈136 bytes OK; NOT NULL + default values: id has no default value but the primary key is usually AUTO_INCREMENT (here there is no AUTO_INCREMENT, but the spec requires required fields include id; not having a default value on the primary key is generally acceptable — \"fields should be set to NOT NULL with a default value as much as possible