xtra_field1, extra_field2, extra_field3 — indeed undefined. Similarly Table 2: the table defines fields id...del_flag (11 fields), and the index references extra_field — undefined. Confirmed.\n\nHmm, wait. This test case may have intentional design: the extra_field references may be a \"script-unfriendly\" trap testing whether I will also report non-existent fields when reporting \"too many composite index fields\". As a database review expert I must report it. OK.\n\nRegarding the final DETECTED category order: report in table order or by issue importance. I follow the body-text order:\nRedundant index (duplicate build on primary-key id); index referencing non-existent field; too many composite index fields; unique index naming not compliant; normal index naming not compliant; index name too long\n\nHmm, \"index referencing non-existent field\" is not a clause in the spec documents, but it is a decidable error. Put it in DETECTED.\n\nOne more consideration: should the three \"redundant index\" instances be merged into one category \"redundant index\"? Yes. DETECTED should be rule categories, not each instance.\n\nFinal DETECTED:\nDETECTED: redundant index (duplicate build on primary-key id); index referencing non-existent field; too many composite index fields; unique index naming not compliant; normal index naming not compliant; index name too long\n\nIs \"unique index naming not compliant\" and \"normal index naming not compliant\" to be merged into \"index naming not compliant\"? I keep them separate for precision. Hmm... the example gives \"index naming not compliant\". Merging to one would be \"index naming not compliant\". But the details distinguish uk_/ix_ prefixes. I think splitting is better because two independent issues were fixed.\n\nOK, now write the body text. The user's rules also require all responses in two parts (direct execution / deep interaction). Language: Simplified Chinese.\n\nBody outline:\n\n## 1. Direct execution: review report\n\nTable-