dex/duplicate build on primary key id (redundant index)\n2. Too many composite index fields (>5) (too many composite index fields)\n3. Unique index naming not compliant (not uk_) (unique index naming)\n4. Normal index naming not compliant (not ix_) (normal index naming)\n5. Index name too long (index name too long)\nPlus possibly: index referencing non-existent field (non-existent field).\n\nRegarding the extra_field issue: in Table 1, the ix_extra1 (extra_field1) field is undefined. In Table 2, KEY ix_multi_cols (order_no, buyer_id, product_id, shop_id, category_id, extra_field) — extra_field is also undefined. This is a DDL that cannot be executed at all. As a review expert, I must report it. This is decidable (field list vs index list comparison). Category: \"index referencing non-existent field\".\n\nHmm, but it could also be that the test case author intended these indexes only as illustrations. But from a spec review perspective, it is a real error. I will report it.\n\nLet me count Table 2's ix_multi_cols fields: order_no, buyer_id, product_id, shop_id, category_id, extra_field = 6 fields > 5. VIOLATION.\n\nTable 2 other checks:\n- Required fields complete, del_flag OK, naming OK.\n- Index naming OK (ix_multi_cols starts with ix_).\n\nTable 3 t_order_bad_uk:\n- UNIQUE KEY uk_order_no (order_no) OK.\n- UNIQUE KEY unique_buyer_id (buyer_id): does not start with uk_. VIOLATION: unique index naming.\n- KEY ix_buyer_id (buyer_id): buyer_id already has a unique index unique_buyer_id; building ix_buyer_id again is a redundant index. VIOLATION: redundant index. Hmm, the file comment only mentions the naming issue. But it is indeed a duplicate build on the same field. I can report it as a redundant index issue.\n\nTable 4 t_order_bad_ix:\n- KEY idx_buyer_id (buyer_id), KEY idx_create_time (create_time): do not start with ix_. VIOLATION: normal index naming.\n\nTable 5 t_order_long_index:\n- KEY ix_this_is_a_very_long_index_name_that_exceeds_sixty_four_characters (buye